<title>FRANCE: Euro Commission publishes enlargement assessments.</title>
<headline>Euro Commission publishes enlargement assessments.</headline>
<dateline>STRASBOURG, France 1997-07-16</dateline>
<text>
<p>The European Commission on Wednesday officially published assessments of how suited 10 eastern European candidates are for EU entry.</p>
<p>The documents, details of which were leaked in advance, recommend that the Czech Republic, Poland, Hungary, Slovenia and Estonia join Cyprus for a first wave of entry talks next year.  </p>
<p>The documents, to be officially presented to the European Parliament by Commission President Jacques Santer, also say that Latvia, Lithuania, Slovakia, Bulgaria and Romania should be kept in what amounts to a waiting room where they can prepare for membership at their own pace.</p>
<p>The Commission also lays down a list of policy challenges faced by the EU to prepare for its new members.</p>
<p>They include a radical reform of the EU's Common Agricultural Policy and a new intergovernmental conference to loosen up the EU's decision-making procedures for the day it has new members.</p>
<p>The five former Communist bloc countries join Cyprus,, which has already been judged suitable for EU talks and has been in the queue for some time.</p>
</text>
